{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":569.6500244140625,"text":"for your latency for your GPUs. And the red line is what we're optimizing for. That's the time to first token. So when we started back in April, you could see records per second around three, which is high, but not so high, and time to first token over 10 seconds, which is very slow. A lot of the data that you see is the same number of GPU. We didn't increase GPU count. We stayed at 56. And by the end, you see in August, records per second were over seven, and time to first token were around 1.6. So we've had 88% improvement in performance for 76% increased demand. So learning from this is there's a lot of parameters that you can improve on, and you really need to resist scaling if you're looking to self-host a model. So let's talk parameters. There's no secret recipe, but the things to consider are the following. First one, long context is a scam. You really need to be mindful that even though you can serve one million token, 256, 256 is really what you need. Contestation, if you're running black wall generation GPU, you don't want to run anything higher than FP4. And the FP4 is best. Roughly going from FP4 to FP8 is a 40% saving in GPU footprint for zero loss in precision. Other parameters to consider, tensor parallelism. Even though GLM fits on TP4, you don't have enough KV cache. And so maybe a TP8 works better, depending on how large your workload is. And then on the more esoteric, check your cache hit. Coding test needs to be above 90% cache hit. And you can look into optimize spec decoding, sequential pre-fill, PDDZAG, all of these. Cool, so now let's talk about production setup. GLM, obviously, is a text-only model, which is a limitation for some people. The trade-off, I would say, we would make every time is get the smarter model and figure out how to do image recognition another way. You can easily have a local router. OpenCodecs is one where you would route any image recognition to Gemini or Luna and keep GLM for the main task. If you really need some vision model, GLM 5G Flash works really well. We have our UI designer making great use of it. Turnaround for UI product has gone a really, really long way. And then the last one is going to be about the harness. So all of these endpoints are going to be OpenAI-compatible API with response API. And so you can use them inside OpenCodecs. Codecs app, as well, works very well. And so that's what we recommend. And then last message, this one is for the CIO in the room. There's been a lot of stigma in using Chinese open source model. So the thing to consider is you're not using the inference dedicated endpoint that goes to China. You're just getting the weights and putting them on a US-based service. So for data sharing side, this is totally fine. Revolute, in particular, because we're a EU-regulated bank, we fall under the most strict regime in terms of compliance. And so what we find that works is all of these coding tasks are not considered autonomous agents. They're always with a human in the loop. And so how do you give reassurance that you have a human in the loop? You start by creating a sandbox that guarantees that. And the sandbox means now, if you've used Codecs load code, it has all of these features of approve for me. You just have to set the rules for the operations that you don't want to allow. So you can allow grep, but not gradle, for instance. And that's going to give confidence to your regulator that it's not an autonomous agent that needs different supervision. The sandbox also protects you from rogue use. You may have a bad actor inside your company, and you want to shield for that. You'd be surprised what you find in a large company. And then the last one is prompt injection. Not so much a thing now, given how smart models are, but always a thing. In terms of data privacy, we spoke about it. So as long as the data sits in one of the countries that are in the data equivalency framework, so US, EU, UK, Israel, you're fine. You can get your GPUs there. All of your inference provider or GPU provider are going to have zero data retention by default. And then the last thing you need is a very strong open telemetry, so that if you ever have an incident, you need to be able to go deep and look at the logs. Also gives you some analytics. All right, thanks, everyone. That was all for my talk. Thank you. 字幕視聴ありがとうございました。","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":0.0,"end":2.799999952316284,"text":" for your latency for your GPUs.","tokens":[50364,337,428,27043,337,428,18407,82,13,50504],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.29760828614234924,"compression_ratio":1.5942622423171997,"no_speech_prob":0.14590515196323395},{"id":1,"seek":0,"start":2.799999952316284,"end":4.71999979019165,"text":" And the red line is what we're optimizing for.","tokens":[50504,400,264,2182,1622,307,437,321,434,40425,337,13,50600],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.29760828614234924,"compression_ratio":1.5942622423171997,"no_speech_prob":0.14590515196323395},{"id":2,"seek":0,"start":4.71999979019165,"end":7.639999866485596,"text":" That's the time to first token.","tokens":[50600,663,311,264,565,281,700,14862,13,50746],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.29760828614234924,"compression_ratio":1.5942622423171997,"no_speech_prob":0.14590515196323395},{"id":3,"seek":0,"start":7.639999866485596,"end":9.920000076293945,"text":" So when we started back in April,","tokens":[50746,407,562,321,1409,646,294,6929,11,50860],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.29760828614234924,"compression_ratio":1.5942622423171997,"no_speech_prob":0.14590515196323395},{"id":4,"seek":0,"start":9.920000076293945,"end":13.920000076293945,"text":" you could see records per second around three, which","tokens":[50860,291,727,536,7724,680,1150,926,1045,11,597,51060],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.29760828614234924,"compression_ratio":1.5942622423171997,"no_speech_prob":0.14590515196323395},{"id":5,"seek":0,"star